<h2 id="structs">Structs, methods and ref this</h2>
<p>A Bayan struct is a value, like a Go struct. Fields are written type first, as in a declaration. The methods live inside the struct instead of beside it. A Go pointer receiver (<code>*Stack</code>) becomes <code>ref this</code>: it says the method changes the struct. A method without it can only read.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">type Stack struct{ items []int }

func (s *Stack) Push(x int) { s.items = append(s.items, x) }

func (s *Stack) Pop() (int, bool) {
    if len(s.items) == 0 {
        return 0, false
    }
    last := s.items[len(s.items)-1]
    s.items = s.items[:len(s.items)-1]
    return last, true
}</code></pre>
<example name="guide-go-basics"/>
<p><code>Pop</code> returns an <code>i64?</code> where Go returns a pair. The caller cannot ignore the "was there one" half, because the type does not let it use the value until it has dealt with <code>null</code>. Here <code>?? -1</code> supplies a fallback for the empty stack.</p>
<p>A struct literal must set every field that has no default. Leave one out and the compiler stops: <code>P needs a value for y</code> (BYN2109). Go fills in zero values silently, and Bayan does not.</p>

<h2 id="ref">Pointers become ref and spans</h2>
<p>There are no pointers. You pass a variable by <code>ref</code> when the callee must change it, and you write <code>ref</code> at the call too, so the change is visible where it happens. For reading part of a collection without copying it, use a span, which is a view like a Go slice but cannot grow.</p>
<example name="guide-go-ref"/>
<p><code>total(xs.span[1..3])</code> sums the elements at positions 1 and 2. The range excludes its end, as in Go. A <code>ReadOnlySpan</code> parameter takes a view of a list, an array or part of either, and the callee cannot change it.</p>

<h2 id="errors">Errors without if err != nil</h2>
<p>A Go function returns <code>(T, error)</code> and every call is followed by a check. A Bayan function returns <code>T</code> and lists what it can fail with. The check is <code>try</code>: if the call fails, the function returns the same failure. <code>catch</code> handles it where you are.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">func readPort(text string) (int, error) {
    if text == "" {
        return 0, ErrMissing
    }
    n, err := strconv.Atoi(text)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, ErrBadPort
    }
    return n, nil
}

func doubled(text string) (int, error) {
    port, err := readPort(text)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    return port * 2, nil
}</code></pre>
<example name="guide-go-errors"/>
<p>The error type is a closed list of cases, not an open <code>error</code> interface. <code>catch switch</code> must cover every case, so adding a case to <code>ConfigError</code> breaks every caller that does not handle it. In Go you would find out with a <code>switch</code> that falls to <code>default</code>, or not at all. There is no wrapping and no <code>errors.Is</code>: the case itself is what you match on. Errors can also carry data. See <a href="errors.html">Errors</a>.</p>

<h2 id="interfaces">Interfaces are declared, not discovered</h2>
<p>A Go type satisfies an interface by having the methods. A Bayan type says so: <code>struct Square : IShape</code>. Interface names start with <code>I</code>. A generic function names the interface it needs with <code>where T : IShape</code>, and the compiler checks the type at the call.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">type Shape interface {
    Area() float64
    Name() string
}

func Total[T Shape](shapes []T) float64 {
    sum := 0.0
    for _, s := range shapes {
        sum += s.Area()
    }
    return sum
}</code></pre>
<example name="guide-go-iface"/>
<p><code>name</code> is a property in the interface (no parentheses), so <code>r.name</code> reads like a field. If a type has the methods but not the <code>: IShape</code>, passing it to <code>total</code> is an error:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2807:</span> `S` does not conform to `IShape`: missing `area`
  --&gt; conform.byn:10:14

  10 │     print($"{total(S { a: 1.0 })}")
     │              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the generic code needs this of its type arguments
     │
  help: add `: IShape` to `S` and provide `area`

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2807</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><b>Why:</b> a method that happens to share a name with an interface method is not a promise. A declaration is a promise, and it is where a reader looks to find every type that implements an interface.</p>
<p>Generics are checked once, at the definition. Inside <code>total</code>, a <code>T</code> can only do what its bound allows. A bare <code>T</code> with no bound can be copied, compared with <code>==</code>, passed and returned, nothing more: there is no <code>constraints.Ordered</code> to reach for yet, so a generic <code>max</code> is not writable today.</p>

<h2 id="defer">defer</h2>
<p><code>defer</code> works as in Go: the statement runs when the scope ends, last registered first. One difference: the scope is the enclosing block, not the whole function. A <code>defer</code> inside a loop body runs at the end of each pass, not when the function returns.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">func work(name string) {
    fmt.Println("open", name)
    defer fmt.Println("close", name)
    fmt.Println("use", name)
}</code></pre>
<example name="guide-go-defer"/>
<p>Two modifiers do what you would do by hand with a flag: <code>defer failing</code> runs only if the function fails, and <code>defer panicking</code> only during a panic. A deferred statement cannot fail.</p>

<h2 id="slices">Slices, strings and maps</h2>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">mid := xs[1:3]
fmt.Println(s[:5])
counts := map[string]int{}
for _, w := range strings.Fields("a b a c a b") {
    counts[w]++
}</code></pre>
<example name="guide-go-slice"/>
<p>A <code>List</code> is not a slice. It owns its storage, so there is no capacity, no aliasing between two lists, and no "append may or may not reallocate" surprise. A span is the slice-like view, and it borrows. A <code>Map</code> iterates in insertion order. A string is immutable UTF-8 text, and like Go's it indexes by byte.</p>

<h2 id="surprises">What will surprise you</h2>

<h3 id="s-nil">Reading a missing key stops the program</h3>
<p>In Go <code>m["x"]</code> gives the zero value. In Bayan it panics with <code>key not found in map</code>, the same way <code>xs[9]</code> panics with <code>index out of range</code>. Use <code>tryGet(at:)</code> when absence is normal. <b>Why:</b> a zero value cannot be told apart from a stored zero.</p>

<h3 id="s-overflow">Integers do not wrap</h3>
<p>Go wraps on overflow. A Bayan integer panics with <code>arithmetic overflow</code>, and a narrowing conversion is written out (<code>i32(a)</code>). A value converts to a wider type on its own, but never to a narrower one. Dividing by zero panics too.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">panic:</span> arithmetic overflow
  at overflow.byn:3</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="s-copy">A list is moved or copied by name</h3>
<p><code>b := a</code> on a slice shares memory. In Bayan, <code>var b = a</code> on a <code>List</code> does not compile:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2780:</span> implicit copy of a unique value: `List&lt;i64&gt;` is unique: it owns its storage
  --&gt; copy.byn:3:13

  3 │     var b = a
    │             ^
    │
  help: hand the value over with `move a`; make an independent copy with `a.copy(to: alloc)`; pass
        it by `ref` to share it instead
    │ 3 │     var b = move a
    │   │             +++++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2780</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Use <code>move a</code> to give the list away, <code>a.copy()</code> for another one, or <code>ref</code> to share it. Plain numbers and booleans copy freely. <b>Why:</b> two names for one buffer is how Go programs get data races and spooky edits. See <a href="memory.html">Memory and ownership</a>.</p>

<h3 id="s-unused">A value you drop is an error</h3>
<p>Go lets you call a function and ignore its result, and rejects an unused variable. Bayan does the reverse for results:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3201:</span> the value of this `load()` is unused
  --&gt; unused.byn:3:5

  3 │     load()
    │     ^^^^^^ value dropped
    │
  help: assign it with `let _ = …`, or remove the expression

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN3201</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Write <code>let _ = load()</code> to say you mean it. A <code>var</code> that is never reassigned is a warning that asks for <code>let</code>.</p>

<h3 id="s-immutable">Bindings are fixed by default</h3>
<p><code>let</code> cannot be reassigned, and a <code>ref this</code> method cannot be called on it. <code>var</code> is the exception you mark. Parameters are always immutable: copy to a <code>var</code> inside the function if you need to change one.</p>

<h3 id="s-switch">switch is exhaustive and does not fall through</h3>
<p>Go's <code>switch</code> stops at the end of a case already. Bayan's <code>switch</code> over a union or an enum must also cover every case, or the program does not build. There is no <code>default</code> to hide behind unless you write one.</p>

<h3 id="s-concurrency">No goroutines yet</h3>
<note>Planned: structured concurrency with <code>async</code> and <code>await</code> at the call site. The compiler rejects both today. Channels and mutexes are named in the specification and have no methods yet. See the <a href="cookbook-concurrency.html">Concurrency cookbook</a>.</note>
<p>The design differs from <code>go f()</code>: a function is not declared async, a task cannot outlive the block that started it, and leaving the block cancels unfinished tasks.</p>

<h3 id="s-names">Names and labels</h3>
<p>Types are <code>PascalCase</code> and everything else is <code>camelCase</code>, whether public or not. There is no exported-by-capital rule: visibility is a keyword (<code>public</code> for other packages, nothing for the module, <code>private</code> for the type). A directory is a module. Arguments after the first carry a label such as <code>by:</code> or <code>from:</code>. See <a href="parameters.html">Parameters and arguments</a>.</p>
<p>Next: <a href="effective.html">Effective Bayan</a> says which form to choose.</p>
